 "eras": [
  {"id":"1568","year":"1568","label":"Akbar's siege","short":"1568","lens":"none","summary":"The mesa fort when Akbar took it in 1568, shown as the present rampart, gates, tanks and a few monuments, all carried back.","basis":"Britannica: sieges by Alauddin Khalji (1303), Bahadur Shah of Gujarat (1534-35) and Akbar (1567-68, captured 1568). The drawn fort is today's mapped line, carried back, marked inferred.","conjecture":"No dated plan of Chittorgarh was found. Walls, gates and tanks are carried back unchanged; only Rana Kumbha's palace, the Victory Tower and Kalika Mata temple are kept of the buildings, and even their 1568 state is not established.","sources":["CHI-01","CHI-19"],"facts":["Alauddin Khalji besieged the fort in 1303, Bahadur Shah of Gujarat in 1534-35 and Akbar in 1567-68, taking it in 1568 (Britannica).","Britannica dates the Tower of Fame to the 12th century and the Tower of Victory to the 15th; no height is given there."]},
  {"id":"today","year":"Today","label":"ASI monument","short":"Monument","lens":"none","summary":"A World Heritage hill fort (Hill Forts of Rajasthan, 2013) on a mesa about 180 m above the plain, with palaces, temples, tanks and a living ascent road.","basis":"OSM outline, rampart, gates, tanks, monuments and roads, checked against the 2010 ASI guide board, plus three tanks and the Kirti Stambh symbol from the board.","conjecture":"Board elements are +-30 m. Heights are unknown (kerb-high) in this evidence layer; see the constructed layer. The dimensions in written sources (13 km circumference, 280 ha) do not match the mapped ring (see the pass document).","sources":["CHI-01","CHI-18","CHI-22"],"facts":["Inscribed on the World Heritage list in 2013 with other Rajasthan hill forts (Britannica).","The fort rises about 180 m above the surrounding plain (Britannica, approximate); the DEM here gives a 180 m range."]}
 ],
